## 1. Objective and scope

Develop and test a state-space model explaining persistent tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.

The study will:

- Quantify variation attributable to:
  - feedstock and environmental conditions,
  - printer, nozzle, and build location,
  - commanded and realized process conditions,
  - evolving machine state,
  - specimen geometry and internal structure,
  - conditioning and test procedure.
- Separate persistent latent process states from transient noise and measurement error.
- Determine which measurable factors explain practically meaningful strength differences.
- distinguish competing mechanisms through interventions and predicted time-series signatures.
- Apply to one polymer/process/specimen standard initially; broader material and geometry generalization requires a subsequent study.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength.  
Secondary outcomes: yield strength, elastic modulus, strain at break, toughness, failure location, and failure mode.

---

## 2. Assumptions

### State-space representation

For coupon \(i\), printed at time \(t\), model a latent manufacturing state \(x_t\):

\[
x_t = F x_{t-1} + G u_t + w_t
\]

\[
m_i = Hx_{t(i)} + Bz_i + e_i
\]

\[
y_i = \alpha + \beta^\top m_i + \gamma^\top z_i
      + \lambda^\top x_{t(i)} + b_{\text{batch}}
      + b_{\text{printer}} + v_i
\]

where:

- \(u_t\): observed process inputs and deliberate interventions;
- \(x_t\): latent, time-evolving machine/material state;
- \(m_i\): measured specimen properties or mediators;
- \(z_i\): fixed design, conditioning, and testing covariates;
- \(y_i\): tensile response;
- \(w_t,e_i,v_i\): process, measurement, and specimen-level disturbances.

Candidate latent-state components include:

- effective melt temperature,
- extrusion/flow efficiency,
- nozzle condition or partial obstruction,
- thermal-field stability,
- bed adhesion/warpage tendency,
- moisture burden in the active feedstock path,
- interlayer bonding quality,
- machine alignment or motion quality.

The model may be nonlinear if diagnostics support it.

### Scientific assumptions

- Coupon order and timestamps are retained; observations are not treated as exchangeable.
- “Nominally identical” means the same design file and nominal settings, not necessarily identical realized geometry, thermal history, moisture exposure, or test alignment.
- Tensile tests are destructive, so machine state cannot be repeatedly observed through the same coupon; replicate coupons provide noisy observations of nearby states.
- Some factors act directly on strength; others act through density, void structure, dimensions, crystallinity, orientation, or interlayer adhesion.
- Printer drift, environmental variation, and feedstock history may be autocorrelated.
- Measurement systems can create apparent persistence and therefore require independent calibration and repeatability assessment.
- Interventions are randomized where operationally possible. Nonrandom temporal associations alone will not establish causality.
- Practical significance will be defined in advance, for example as a change of at least 5% in mean strength or another engineering threshold justified by the application.

---

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement-system qualification and pilot

Before the main study:

1. Verify load-cell calibration, extensometer performance, alignment, grip pressure, and software calculations.
2. Conduct repeatability and reproducibility studies for:
   - dimensional measurements,
   - mass and density,
   - moisture measurements,
   - thermal sensing,
   - image-based void metrics,
   - tensile outcomes where duplicate or reference materials permit.
3. Run a short chronological pilot to estimate:
   - within-build and between-build variance,
   - temporal autocorrelation,
   - likely state persistence,
   - destructive-test sample size requirements,
   - sensor synchronization reliability.
4. Use pilot estimates for simulation-based power and identifiability analysis of the proposed state-space model.

Pilot coupons will not enter the confirmatory dataset unless inclusion is prespecified.

### Phase B: Baseline longitudinal production

Print a continuous sequence of standardized tensile coupons over multiple days and builds, using:

- at least three printers of the same model, if available;
- at least three feedstock spools or lots;
- multiple build-plate positions;
- multiple operators or operator-independent automated procedures;
- repeated reference settings and periodic reference coupons.

Record every coupon’s print order, exact location, neighboring coupons, and elapsed machine operating time. Sample densely enough to resolve short-term drift, including consecutive coupons within builds and repeated builds across days.

Randomize, subject to safety and process constraints:

- printer-to-spool assignment,
- specimen position and orientation,
- order of nominal process settings,
- conditioning and tensile-test order,
- testing operator and machine, if more than one is used.

Use blocking by day, printer, feedstock lot, and build. Preserve chronological order in the data even when test order is randomized.

### Phase C: Randomized mechanism interventions

Use a factorial or fractional-factorial design with repeated crossovers. Candidate manipulated factors are:

- feedstock moisture: controlled dry versus controlled humidified condition;
- nozzle state: new/cleaned versus defined service age;
- nozzle temperature: low, center, and high within the qualified range;
- cooling airflow: standardized levels;
- print speed or volumetric flow: qualified levels;
- chamber/ambient temperature and humidity;
- build orientation and raster orientation;
- bed position;
- machine maintenance/reset intervention;
- conditioning duration and humidity before tensile testing;
- tensile speed, grip protocol, and alignment check within the governing standard.

Not all factors need simultaneous manipulation. Select a design that estimates main effects and prespecified interactions while limiting aliasing, particularly:

- moisture × temperature,
- speed × temperature,
- cooling × orientation,
- nozzle age × volumetric flow,
- build position × printer,
- conditioning × test day.

Include reversal or washout sequences such as dry–wet–dry, old-nozzle–new-nozzle, and baseline–maintenance–baseline. These distinguish reversible environmental states from persistent wear or machine-specific effects.

### Phase D: Confirmatory replication

Lock the model structure and key hypotheses after exploratory analysis. Conduct an independent confirmatory production campaign using:

- new print dates,
- fresh randomized assignments,
- at least one new feedstock lot,
- preferably an additional printer or laboratory.

Reserve this phase for out-of-sample prediction, intervention-effect confirmation, and variance-reduction validation.

### Competing explanations and distinguishing observations

1. **Feedstock moisture**
   - Prediction: moisture sensor/mass-loss measurements precede strength changes; drying improves strength; humidification reverses the improvement; effects may include increased voids or surface defects.
   - Contrary evidence: persistent printer-specific differences after moisture equalization and dry–wet crossover.

2. **Nozzle wear or partial blockage**
   - Prediction: strength changes gradually with service time or abruptly after obstruction; extrusion mass/width and pressure signatures change; nozzle replacement resets the state.
   - Contrary evidence: no reset after blinded replacement and no association with flow metrics.

3. **Thermal-control drift**
   - Prediction: realized temperature trajectories, not commanded setpoints, explain changes; independent thermometry and thermal-field measurements predict bonding and strength; recalibration or stabilization restores performance.
   - Contrary evidence: strength differences remain after verified equal thermal histories.

4. **Spatial thermal or cooling gradients**
   - Prediction: reproducible build-plate position patterns rotate or move when airflow, coupon layout, or machine coordinates are changed.
   - Contrary evidence: position effects disappear when test order—but not print position—is randomized, indicating testing drift instead.

5. **Geometry, density, or void differences**
   - Prediction: mass, cross-section, porosity, bead geometry, and interlayer contacts mediate most strength variation; adjusting for these properties attenuates printer/time effects.
   - Contrary evidence: strength differences persist among coupons matched on these measurements.

6. **Crystallinity or molecular orientation**
   - Prediction: thermal history and material characterization correlate with strength; controlled annealing or cooling interventions alter both structure and tensile response.
   - Contrary evidence: structural measures remain constant despite strength shifts.

7. **Testing-system or conditioning artifacts**
   - Prediction: effects follow tensile machine, operator, load-cell calibration, test order, specimen moisture, grip alignment, or strain-rate assignment rather than print chronology; reference coupons show similar shifts.
   - Contrary evidence: randomized test order and independent testing reproduce print-time patterns.

8. **Unmeasured persistent machine state**
   - Prediction: residuals remain autocorrelated and printer-specific after observed factors are included; intervention responses reveal state resets or slow recovery.
   - Interpretation: evidence of a latent process state, but not identification of its physical mechanism until targeted measurements account for it.

---

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Manufacturing records

- printer identifier, firmware, maintenance, calibration, and cumulative operating hours;
- slicer version and full machine-readable parameter file;
- design-file checksum and toolpath file;
- coupon print order, timestamp, build position, and orientation;
- nozzle material, diameter, service age, replacement, and cleaning events;
- commanded temperature, speed, acceleration, extrusion multiplier, layer height, line width, cooling, chamber settings, and bed temperature;
- interruptions, alarms, jams, operator actions, and failed prints.

### High-frequency process data

Time-synchronized records of:

- actual nozzle, bed, and chamber temperature;
- ambient temperature and relative humidity;
- motor current, extrusion pressure or suitable proxy;
- filament feed rate and encoder discrepancy;
- print-head velocity;
- cooling-fan status;
- power consumption where informative;
- in situ imaging or thermal imaging where feasible.

Retain raw trajectories rather than only build averages.

### Feedstock data

- polymer grade, color/additives, lot, spool, manufacture date, and storage history;
- filament diameter and ovality along the spool;
- moisture content before, during, and after printing;
- drying and humidification protocol;
- thermal or rheological properties on a planned subset.

### Coupon evidence

- dimensions at multiple gauge locations;
- mass and apparent density;
- warpage and surface roughness;
- bead width, layer height, neck formation, and dimensional deviations;
- void fraction and morphology from nondestructive imaging on a representative subset;
- fracture-surface images and classified failure modes;
- crystallinity or related material-state measurements where relevant;
- conditioning duration, temperature, humidity, and post-print age.

### Tensile-test evidence

- test-machine, load-cell, grips, extensometer, software version, and calibration status;
- operator, test order, date/time, strain rate, grip pressure, alignment check;
- raw force, displacement, and strain time series;
- cross-sectional area calculation;
- ultimate strength, yield strength, modulus, elongation, toughness;
- failure location and validity against the selected test standard.

### Quality-control evidence

- reference material or reference coupons tested at fixed intervals;
- duplicate measurements and blinded remeasurements;
- sensor calibration records;
- exclusions, missingness, protocol deviations, and reasons for reruns.

---

## 5. Measurement

### Primary outcome

Ultimate tensile strength calculated using a prespecified cross-sectional-area convention. Report both standard nominal-area and measured-area results if the governing standard permits, because dimensional variation may otherwise be conflated with material strength.

### Secondary outcomes

- yield strength;
- modulus;
- strain at break;
- toughness;
- peak load;
- valid gauge-region failure indicator;
- fracture mode.

### Process-state indicators

Create calibrated, physically interpretable summaries from raw time series, including:

- mean, variance, overshoot, and settling behavior of nozzle and bed temperatures;
- spatial and temporal thermal gradients;
- extrusion-rate error;
- pressure/current anomalies;
- cumulative nozzle service and recent extrusion volume;
- elapsed time since drying, maintenance, start-up, pause, or material change;
- ambient and feedstock moisture trajectories.

Summaries must be defined before examining tensile outcomes. Raw sensor data remain available for alternative observation models.

### Specimen mediators

Measure:

- cross-sectional dimensions and variation;
- mass and density;
- void fraction and void orientation;
- surface roughness and warpage;
- deposited-road dimensions;
- interlayer contact geometry;
- crystallinity or analogous polymer-state indicators.

Use a stratified subset for expensive measurements, oversampling extreme and typical process states without selecting on tensile outcome alone.

### Measurement reliability

- Estimate repeatability and reproducibility for each critical measure.
- Blind metrology and fracture classification to printer, treatment, and tensile outcome when feasible.
- Use independent temperature sensing to assess controller bias.
- Synchronize clocks to a common time base.
- Flag sensor saturation, dropout, and calibration drift.
- Predefine rules for invalid tensile failures, but retain invalid results for sensitivity analyses rather than silently deleting them.

---

## 6. Analysis

### State-space model

Fit a hierarchical dynamic model with:

- printer-specific latent states;
- shared environmental inputs;
- feedstock/spool states;
- intervention effects on state transitions;
- coupon-level observations of geometry, structure, and tensile response;
- batch, build, plate-position, and testing-system effects;
- explicit measurement-error models.

Allow latent-state persistence to vary by mechanism. For example:

- moisture may evolve according to exposure and drying inputs;
- nozzle condition may follow cumulative usage with resets at replacement;
- thermal stability may show warm-up transients and short-memory fluctuations.

Compare linear-Gaussian models with robust or nonlinear alternatives if residuals, intervention responses, or physical constraints require them. Avoid adding latent dimensions without evidence; assess observability and parameter identifiability using simulation and posterior diagnostics.

### Variance attribution

Partition tensile-strength variance into:

- observed fixed factors;
- printer and feedstock effects;
- build and plate-position effects;
- latent persistent state;
- specimen-level residual variation;
- tensile measurement error.

Report uncertainty intervals for each component and the proportion of out-of-sample variance explained.

### Temporal diagnostics

Assess:

- autocorrelation and cross-correlation;
- change points;
- warm-up and recovery dynamics;
- state half-lives;
- pre/post-intervention level and slope changes;
- whether residual temporal structure remains after modeling measured factors.

Chronological holdout prediction is required; random cross-validation alone is inappropriate for persistent states.

### Mechanism and mediation analysis

For randomized interventions:

1. estimate total intervention effects on strength;
2. estimate effects on proposed mediators;
3. evaluate whether mediator inclusion attenuates the intervention effect;
4. retain direct and mediated effects with uncertainty.

Causal mediation claims will require temporal ordering, intervention relevance, and explicit assumptions about mediator–outcome confounding. Otherwise, describe mediation as associational.

### Model comparisons

Compare prespecified models representing:

- independent coupon noise only;
- static printer/batch effects;
- observed-factor regression;
- latent dynamic machine state;
- dynamic moisture state;
- wear/reset state;
- testing-system drift;
- combined models.

Use predictive log score or expected predictive accuracy, calibration, residual whiteness, and intervention-response accuracy. Prefer the simplest model meeting predictive and diagnostic requirements.

### Tests distinguishing explanations

Evidence for a mechanism requires convergence of:

- correct temporal ordering;
- association with a calibrated process measure;
- predicted response to randomized intervention;
- reversal, reset, or dose response where expected;
- replication in the confirmatory campaign;
- reduction of latent-state variance after adding the mechanism.

A factor that predicts strength but fails intervention or reversal tests will be treated as a marker rather than an established cause.

### Multiplicity, missingness, and robustness

- Prespecify primary factors, interactions, outcomes, and contrasts.
- Control false discovery for secondary screening analyses.
- Model missing sensor values under stated assumptions and report missingness patterns.
- Conduct sensitivity analyses for invalid failures, area definitions, outliers, sensor bias, prior choices, and unmeasured common causes.
- Report effect sizes and uncertainty, not only significance tests.

---

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Determine the sample size before the main campaign through simulation under plausible state persistence, measurement error, clustering, attrition, and intervention effects. Require adequate power or posterior precision for the predefined practically important strength difference.

Stop data collection only when all prespecified conditions are met:

1. the minimum number of printers, spools/lots, builds, days, interventions, and valid coupons has been completed;
2. each key intervention has sufficient crossover or replication;
3. the primary state-space model is identifiable and diagnostics are acceptable;
4. estimated key effects reach the predefined precision target, such as a 95% interval narrower than the practical-effect threshold;
5. the model is tested on an untouched chronological confirmatory set.

Early stopping is allowed only for:

- safety or equipment risk;
- overwhelming evidence of protocol invalidity;
- a prespecified sequential boundary for a very large intervention effect;
- demonstrated futility under the planned precision criterion.

A factor will be judged explanatory when it:

- produces a practically meaningful and replicated effect;
- improves out-of-sample prediction;
- explains a material portion of persistent variance;
- generates the predicted temporal/intervention signature;
- leaves substantially reduced residual autocorrelation or latent-state variance.

If persistent differences remain but no measured factor meets these criteria, conclude that an unresolved latent manufacturing state exists and use its inferred timing and reset behavior to design the next targeted study. Do not label the latent state as a specific mechanism without corroborating measurements.

---

## 8. Limitations

- Results may be specific to the selected polymer, printer architecture, nozzle, geometry, slicer, and laboratory environment.
- Latent states can summarize omitted processes without uniquely identifying them.
- Some candidate states may be weakly observable or mutually confounded, especially temperature, flow, and bonding quality.
- Destructive tensile testing limits repeated observation of individual coupons.
- Expensive imaging and material characterization may require subset sampling and reduce mediation precision.
- Sensor installation may alter the process or fail to measure conditions at the polymer–polymer interface.
- Interventions such as drying, nozzle replacement, and thermal adjustment may affect several mechanisms simultaneously.
- Conditioning and testing controls cannot fully eliminate specimen-level heterogeneity.
- Invalid grip or off-gauge failures may cause informative missingness.
- Model conclusions depend on transition and observation assumptions; alternative dynamics must be examined.
- Variance explained in one campaign may not predict long-term behavior after maintenance, firmware changes, material substitutions, or environmental shifts.

